=== loaded: 66 spinners on 22 vCPUs ===
   samples   window_ms    mean_ns    min_ns mean/min      max_ns   abs_ok
     10000        0.23       23.0        20     1.15          47      yes
    100000        2.32       23.2        16     1.45       16903      yes
    500000       43.42       86.8        15     5.79    12014090      yes
   2000000      170.67       85.3        15     5.69    16007605      yes

Two things this shows.

1. The canary only sees contention that happens while it is sampling, so the sample
   count is really a detection window. At 10k samples the window is short enough to
   fit between preemptions and a loaded host can read clean. The engine samples for a
   fixed 8 ms of wall time instead (kCanaryWindow), which pins the window regardless
   of how expensive a call is in this build.

2. mean/min is the contention signal, not the mean alone. The mean is build-dependent
   (a quiet ASan build reads ~144 ns and would trip an absolute 100 ns rule on an idle
   machine); min is this build's own cost, measured between preemptions, so the ratio
   is ~1 on any quiet host and large on a busy one with nothing to calibrate.
